                                                               Using Two Pointers
----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

class Solution {
    public boolean isPalindrome(String s) {
        String st = s.replaceAll("[^a-zA-Z0-9]","").toLowerCase();
         int left =0;
         int right = st.length()-1;

         char[] arr = st.toCharArray();

         while(left<right){
            char ch = arr[left];
            arr[left]= arr[right];
            arr[right]= ch;

            left++;
            right--;

         }
         String stt = new String(arr);
         
         return stt.equals(st);
        
    }
}


The error occurs because String is immutable in Java.
charAt() returns a value, not a variable that you can assign to.
For example:
st.charAt(left) = st.charAt(right);   // ❌
is like writing:
'a' = 'b';    // ❌
You cannot change a character inside a String.


If you want to swap characters, use a char[]
char[] arr = st.toCharArray();
------------------------------------
char temp = arr[left];
arr[left] = arr[right];
arr[right] = temp;

st = new String(arr);
Or use StringBuilder
-------------------------------------------------
StringBuilder sb = new StringBuilder(st);
char temp = sb.charAt(left);
sb.setCharAt(left, sb.charAt(right));
sb.setCharAt(right, temp);

st = sb.toString();













